IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `cpp-core`
IMPORT `cr1-part1-contributions`

-- Tests of cr1-part1-contributions.l4 (Regulations 3 to 7). Every expected value is worked out by hand from the
-- text (C.R.C.-c.-385.txt lines 57 to 135) before the run. THE RATES, THE EXEMPTION AND THE EARNINGS CEILINGS
-- BELOW ARE INVENTED ROUND NUMBERS, NOT THE ACT'S FIGURES: the Act fixes them (piece cp1) and these tests only
-- need some. Combined base and first additional rate 0.06; second additional rate 0.04.
--   year's maximum base contribution              0.05 x (70,000 - 3,500)  = 3,325
--   year's maximum first additional contribution  0.01 x (70,000 - 3,500)  =   665   (together 3,990)
--   year's maximum second additional contribution 0.04 x (80,000 - 70,000) =   400

§ `Tests of regulations 3 to 7`

§§ `Fixtures`

`cr1 test figures` MEANS `cr1 Figures the Act sets for a year` WITH
    `the contribution rate for employees` IS 0.05
    `the first additional contribution rate for employees` IS 0.01
    `the second additional contribution rate for employees` IS 0.04
    `the basic exemption` IS 3500
    `the maximum pensionable earnings` IS 70000
    `the additional maximum pensionable earnings` IS 80000

GIVEN total IS A NUMBER
      ordinary IS A NUMBER
      how IS A `cr1 How an employee is ordinarily paid`
      days IS A NUMBER
      periods IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `cr1 Payment of remuneration`
`cr1 test payment` total ordinary how days periods MEANS `cr1 Payment of remuneration` WITH
    `the payment of remuneration from pensionable employment` IS total
    `the part of the payment that is ordinary remuneration` IS ordinary
    `how the employee is ordinarily paid` IS how
    `the number of days in the pay period` IS days
    `the number of pay periods ending in the year` IS periods

GIVEN deducted IS A NUMBER
      second IS A NUMBER
      paid IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `cr1 Position earlier in the year`
`cr1 test earlier` deducted second paid MEANS `cr1 Position earlier in the year` WITH
    `base and first additional contributions already deducted under this Part` IS deducted
    `second additional contributions already deducted under this Part` IS second
    `total remuneration the employer has already paid the employee in the year` IS paid

`cr1 test no provincial plan` MEANS `cr1 Provincial pension plan position` WITH
    `the employee has made contributions for the year under a provincial pension plan in respect of salary and wages paid by the employer` IS FALSE
    `base and first additional contributions already required to be deducted under the provincial pension plan` IS 800
    `second additional contributions already required to be deducted under the provincial pension plan` IS 100
    `sum of the provincial plan's contribution rate and first additional contribution rate for employees` IS 0.10
    `the provincial plan's second additional contribution rate for employees` IS 0.08

GIVEN flag IS A BOOLEAN
      baseFirst IS A NUMBER
      second IS A NUMBER
      sumRates IS A NUMBER
      secondRate IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `cr1 Provincial pension plan position`
`cr1 test provincial plan` flag baseFirst second sumRates secondRate MEANS `cr1 Provincial pension plan position` WITH
    `the employee has made contributions for the year under a provincial pension plan in respect of salary and wages paid by the employer` IS flag
    `base and first additional contributions already required to be deducted under the provincial pension plan` IS baseFirst
    `second additional contributions already required to be deducted under the provincial pension plan` IS second
    `sum of the provincial plan's contribution rate and first additional contribution rate for employees` IS sumRates
    `the provincial plan's second additional contribution rate for employees` IS secondRate

§§ `Regulation 3: the year's maximum contributions`

#ASSERT `reg 3 — the year's maximum base contribution` `cr1 test figures` EQUALS 3325
#ASSERT `reg 3 — the year's maximum first additional contribution` `cr1 test figures` EQUALS 665
#ASSERT `reg 3 — the year's maximum second additional contribution` `cr1 test figures` EQUALS 400

§§ `Regulation 3: ordinary remuneration, pay day, pay period`

#ASSERT `reg 3 — a payment is ordinary remuneration` TRUE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT `reg 3 — a payment is ordinary remuneration` FALSE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`reg 3 — a payment is ordinary remuneration` FALSE TRUE FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT (`reg 3 — a payment is ordinary remuneration` FALSE FALSE TRUE)

#ASSERT `reg 3 — a day is a pay day` (YearMonthDay 2026 3 13) (YearMonthDay 2026 3 13)
#ASSERT NOT (`reg 3 — a day is a pay day` (YearMonthDay 2026 3 12) (YearMonthDay 2026 3 13))

#ASSERT `reg 3 — the length of the pay period, in days` (JUST 14) 7 EQUALS 14
#ASSERT `reg 3 — the length of the pay period, in days` NOTHING 3 EQUALS 3
#ASSERT `reg 3 — the length of the pay period, in days` NOTHING (1 DIVIDED BY 24) EQUALS (1 DIVIDED BY 24)

§§ `Regulation 5(5): the basic exemption for a pay period`

#ASSERT `reg 5(5) — the employee's basic exemption for a pay period` `paid hourly` 0 EQUALS 1.75
#ASSERT `reg 5(5) — the employee's basic exemption for a pay period` `paid daily` 0 EQUALS 14.58
#ASSERT `reg 5(5) — the employee's basic exemption for a pay period` `paid weekly` 0 EQUALS 67.30
#ASSERT `reg 5(5) — the employee's basic exemption for a pay period` `paid bi-weekly` 0 EQUALS 134.61
#ASSERT `reg 5(5) — the employee's basic exemption for a pay period` `paid quadri-weekly` 0 EQUALS 269.23
#ASSERT `reg 5(5) — the employee's basic exemption for a pay period` `paid semi-monthly` 0 EQUALS 145.83
#ASSERT `reg 5(5) — the employee's basic exemption for a pay period` `paid monthly` 0 EQUALS 291.66
#ASSERT `reg 5(5) — the employee's basic exemption for a pay period` `paid quarterly` 0 EQUALS 875
#ASSERT `reg 5(5) — the employee's basic exemption for a pay period` `paid semi-annually` 0 EQUALS 1750
#ASSERT `reg 5(5) — the employee's basic exemption for a pay period` `paid annually` 0 EQUALS 3500
#ASSERT `reg 5(5) — the employee's basic exemption for a pay period` `paid in 22 payments in respect of a period of 10 months in the year` 0 EQUALS 159.09
#ASSERT `reg 5(5) — the employee's basic exemption for a pay period` `paid semi-monthly in respect of a period of 10 months in the year` 0 EQUALS 175
#ASSERT `reg 5(5) — the employee's basic exemption for a pay period` `paid monthly in respect of a period of 10 months in the year` 0 EQUALS 350
-- (c) any other case: the greater of $3,500 x days/365 and $67.30.
#ASSERT `reg 5(5) — the employee's basic exemption for a pay period` `paid in any other way` 10 EQUALS (35000 DIVIDED BY 365)
#ASSERT `reg 5(5) — the employee's basic exemption for a pay period` `paid in any other way` 5 EQUALS 67.30
-- 7 days is 3,500 x 7/365 = 67.12..., below $67.30; 8 days is 76.71..., above it.
#ASSERT `reg 5(5) — the employee's basic exemption for a pay period` `paid in any other way` 7 EQUALS 67.30
#ASSERT `reg 5(5) — the employee's basic exemption for a pay period` `paid in any other way` 8 EQUALS (28000 DIVIDED BY 365)

§§ `Regulation 5(2): the rounding`

#ASSERT `reg 5(2) — an amount rounded to the nearest multiple of $0.01, or the higher if two are equidistant` 0.015 EQUALS 0.02
#ASSERT `reg 5(2) — an amount rounded to the nearest multiple of $0.01, or the higher if two are equidistant` 0.014 EQUALS 0.01
#ASSERT `reg 5(2) — an amount rounded to the nearest multiple of $0.01, or the higher if two are equidistant` 0.0151 EQUALS 0.02
#ASSERT `reg 5(2) — an amount rounded to the nearest multiple of $0.01, or the higher if two are equidistant` 1.005 EQUALS 1.01
#ASSERT `reg 5(2) — an amount rounded to the nearest multiple of $0.01, or the higher if two are equidistant` 0 EQUALS 0
-- For a negative number the higher of two equidistant multiples is the one nearer zero.
#ASSERT `reg 5(2) — an amount rounded to the nearest multiple of $0.01, or the higher if two are equidistant` (0 MINUS 0.285) EQUALS (0 MINUS 0.28)
#ASSERT `reg 5(2) — an amount rounded to the nearest multiple of $0.01, or the higher if two are equidistant` (0 MINUS 0.286) EQUALS (0 MINUS 0.29)

§§ `Regulation 5(1) and 5(3): the qualifying payment and the rest`

#ASSERT `reg 5(1) — the qualifying payment of remuneration` (`cr1 test payment` 1000 800 `paid weekly` 7 52) EQUALS 800
#ASSERT `reg 5(3) — the portion of a payment that is not a qualifying payment of remuneration` (`cr1 test payment` 1000 800 `paid weekly` 7 52) EQUALS 200

§§ `Regulation 5: the contribution on a payment, no earlier deductions`

-- 1,000 paid weekly, all ordinary: (1,000 - 67.30) x 0.06 = 55.962, rounded 55.96.
#ASSERT `reg 5 — the base and first additional contributions to deduct from a payment` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 1000 1000 `paid weekly` 7 52) (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 0) `cr1 test no provincial plan` EQUALS JUST 55.96
-- 3,000 paid monthly: (3,000 - 291.66) x 0.06 = 162.5004, rounded 162.50.
#ASSERT `reg 5 — the base and first additional contributions to deduct from a payment` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 3000 3000 `paid monthly` 30 12) (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 0) `cr1 test no provincial plan` EQUALS JUST 162.50
-- 800 of the 1,000 ordinary: (800 - 67.30) x 0.06 = 43.962 -> 43.96; the other 200 x 0.06 = 12.00 (subsection (3)).
#ASSERT `reg 5 — the base and first additional contributions to deduct from a payment` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 1000 800 `paid weekly` 7 52) (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 0) `cr1 test no provincial plan` EQUALS JUST 55.96
-- 100 paid hourly: (100 - 1.75) x 0.06 = 5.895, exactly equidistant between 5.89 and 5.90: the higher, 5.90.
#ASSERT `reg 5 — the base and first additional contributions to deduct from a payment` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 100 100 `paid hourly` 0 52) (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 0) `cr1 test no provincial plan` EQUALS JUST 5.90
-- 500 paid on a 10-day period (any other way): the exemption is 7000/73 = 95.89...; (500 - 95.89...) x 0.06 = 24.2465..., rounded 24.25.
#ASSERT `reg 5 — the base and first additional contributions to deduct from a payment` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 500 500 `paid in any other way` 10 36) (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 0) `cr1 test no provincial plan` EQUALS JUST 24.25
-- 67.55 paid weekly: 0.25 x 0.06 = 0.015, equidistant: 0.02.
#ASSERT `reg 5 — the base and first additional contributions to deduct from a payment` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 67.55 67.55 `paid weekly` 7 52) (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 0) `cr1 test no provincial plan` EQUALS JUST 0.02
-- Subsection (7): 67.31 exceeds the weekly exemption 67.30 by one cent, 0.0006 rounds to 0.00, but the contribution is at least $0.01.
#ASSERT `reg 5 — the base and first additional contributions to deduct from a payment` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 67.31 67.31 `paid weekly` 7 52) (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 0) `cr1 test no provincial plan` EQUALS JUST 0.01
-- A payment that only equals the exemption does not "exceed" it: no minimum, and the formula gives nothing.
#ASSERT `reg 5 — the base and first additional contributions to deduct from a payment` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 67.30 67.30 `paid weekly` 7 52) (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 0) `cr1 test no provincial plan` EQUALS JUST 0
-- A payment below the exemption: (50 - 67.30) x 0.06 = -1.038, rounded -1.04; the total is bounded below at nothing (fork F3).
#ASSERT `reg 5 — the base and first additional contributions to deduct from a payment` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 50 50 `paid weekly` 7 52) (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 0) `cr1 test no provincial plan` EQUALS JUST 0

§§ `Regulation 5(6): 27 bi-weekly or 53 weekly pay periods`

-- 1,500 bi-weekly, 27 periods: (1,500 - 134.61) x 0.06 = 81.9234 -> 81.92. Added: 3,500/27 = 129.629..., ignoring
-- amounts under a cent 129.62; 134.61 - 129.62 = 4.99; 4.99 x 0.06 = 0.2994 -> 0.30. Total 82.22.
#ASSERT `reg 5(6) — the amount added in a year with 27 bi-weekly or 53 weekly pay periods` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 1500 1500 `paid bi-weekly` 14 27) EQUALS 0.30
#ASSERT `reg 5 — the base and first additional contributions to deduct from a payment` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 1500 1500 `paid bi-weekly` 14 27) (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 0) `cr1 test no provincial plan` EQUALS JUST 82.22
-- 130 bi-weekly in a 27-period year: (130 - 134.61) x 0.06 = -0.2766 -> -0.28, plus 0.30 = 0.02 (fork F3: the negative
-- amount stays in the sum). Reading the formula as floored at nothing before the add-on would give 0.30.
#ASSERT `reg 5 — the base and first additional contributions to deduct from a payment` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 130 130 `paid bi-weekly` 14 27) (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 0) `cr1 test no provincial plan` EQUALS JUST 0.02
-- 26 bi-weekly periods: nothing is added.
#ASSERT `reg 5 — the base and first additional contributions to deduct from a payment` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 1500 1500 `paid bi-weekly` 14 26) (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 0) `cr1 test no provincial plan` EQUALS JUST 81.92
-- 53 weekly periods: 3,500/53 = 66.037..., ignoring amounts under a cent 66.03; 67.30 - 66.03 = 1.27; 1.27 x 0.06 = 0.0762 -> 0.08.
#ASSERT `reg 5(6) — the amount added in a year with 27 bi-weekly or 53 weekly pay periods` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 1000 1000 `paid weekly` 7 53) EQUALS 0.08
#ASSERT `reg 5 — the base and first additional contributions to deduct from a payment` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 1000 1000 `paid weekly` 7 53) (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 0) `cr1 test no provincial plan` EQUALS JUST 56.04
-- 27 weekly periods, or 53 bi-weekly periods, are not the cases the subsection names.
#ASSERT `reg 5(6) — the amount added in a year with 27 bi-weekly or 53 weekly pay periods` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 1000 1000 `paid weekly` 7 27) EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `reg 5(6) — the amount added in a year with 27 bi-weekly or 53 weekly pay periods` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 1500 1500 `paid bi-weekly` 14 53) EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `reg 5(6) — the amount added in a year with 27 bi-weekly or 53 weekly pay periods` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 3000 3000 `paid monthly` 30 12) EQUALS 0

§§ `Regulation 5(8): the limit on the aggregate`

-- Limit 3,325 + 665 = 3,990. 3,900 already deducted: 90 left, and 162.50 would be due.
#ASSERT `reg 5(8) — the room left under the limit on base and first additional contributions` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test earlier` 3900 0 0) `cr1 test no provincial plan` EQUALS JUST 90
#ASSERT `reg 5 — the base and first additional contributions to deduct from a payment` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 3000 3000 `paid monthly` 30 12) (`cr1 test earlier` 3900 0 0) `cr1 test no provincial plan` EQUALS JUST 90
#ASSERT `reg 5 — the base and first additional contributions to deduct from a payment` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 3000 3000 `paid monthly` 30 12) (`cr1 test earlier` 3000 0 0) `cr1 test no provincial plan` EQUALS JUST 162.50
#ASSERT `reg 5 — the base and first additional contributions to deduct from a payment` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 3000 3000 `paid monthly` 30 12) (`cr1 test earlier` 3989.99 0 0) `cr1 test no provincial plan` EQUALS JUST 0.01
-- Nothing left: the limit governs over the minimum of subsection (7) (fork F4).
#ASSERT `reg 5 — the base and first additional contributions to deduct from a payment` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 3000 3000 `paid monthly` 30 12) (`cr1 test earlier` 3990 0 0) `cr1 test no provincial plan` EQUALS JUST 0
#ASSERT `reg 5(8) — the room left under the limit on base and first additional contributions` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test earlier` 4000 0 0) `cr1 test no provincial plan` EQUALS JUST 0

§§ `Regulation 6: the provincial pension plan`

-- 800 deducted under the provincial plan, whose two rates sum to 0.10, counted at this Act's 0.06: 800 x 0.06/0.10 = 480.
#ASSERT `reg 6(2) — the provincial plan's base and first additional contributions counted at this Act's rates` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test provincial plan` TRUE 800 100 0.10 0.08) EQUALS JUST 480
#ASSERT `reg 6(2) — the provincial plan's base and first additional contributions counted at this Act's rates` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test provincial plan` FALSE 800 100 0.10 0.08) EQUALS JUST 0
#ASSERT `reg 6(2) — the provincial plan's base and first additional contributions counted at this Act's rates` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test provincial plan` TRUE 800 100 0 0.08) EQUALS NOTHING
-- Room: 3,990 - (3,500 deducted under this Part + 480) = 10; the 162.50 due is cut to 10.
#ASSERT `reg 5(8) — the room left under the limit on base and first additional contributions` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test earlier` 3500 0 0) (`cr1 test provincial plan` TRUE 800 100 0.10 0.08) EQUALS JUST 10
#ASSERT `reg 5 — the base and first additional contributions to deduct from a payment` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 3000 3000 `paid monthly` 30 12) (`cr1 test earlier` 3500 0 0) (`cr1 test provincial plan` TRUE 800 100 0.10 0.08) EQUALS JUST 10
-- The same facts with no provincial contributions made: the plan's 800 are ignored, 490 left, 162.50 due.
#ASSERT `reg 5 — the base and first additional contributions to deduct from a payment` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 3000 3000 `paid monthly` 30 12) (`cr1 test earlier` 3500 0 0) (`cr1 test provincial plan` FALSE 800 100 0.10 0.08) EQUALS JUST 162.50
-- The provincial plan's rates sum to nothing: the text does not say, so no answer.
#ASSERT `reg 5 — the base and first additional contributions to deduct from a payment` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 3000 3000 `paid monthly` 30 12) (`cr1 test earlier` 3500 0 0) (`cr1 test provincial plan` TRUE 800 100 0 0.08) EQUALS NOTHING
-- 6(4): 100 x 0.04/0.08 = 50.
#ASSERT `reg 6(4) — the provincial plan's second additional contributions counted at this Act's rate` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test provincial plan` TRUE 800 100 0.10 0.08) EQUALS JUST 50
#ASSERT `reg 6(4) — the provincial plan's second additional contributions counted at this Act's rate` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test provincial plan` FALSE 800 100 0.10 0.08) EQUALS JUST 0
#ASSERT `reg 6(4) — the provincial plan's second additional contributions counted at this Act's rate` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test provincial plan` TRUE 800 100 0.10 0) EQUALS NOTHING

§§ `Regulation 5.1: the second additional contribution`

-- A = 5,000 paid, B = 68,000 already paid: C = the greater of 68,000 and 70,000 = 70,000; E = 73,000 - 70,000 = 3,000.
#ASSERT `reg 5.1(1) — the result of the calculation` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 68000) 5000 EQUALS 3000
-- B = 72,000: C = 72,000; E = 77,000 - 72,000 = 5,000, the whole payment.
#ASSERT `reg 5.1(1) — the result of the calculation` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 72000) 5000 EQUALS 5000
-- B = 60,000: E = 65,000 - 70,000 = -5,000.
#ASSERT `reg 5.1(1) — the result of the calculation` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 60000) 5000 EQUALS (0 MINUS 5000)
#ASSERT `reg 5.1(2) — the second additional contribution on the result` `cr1 test figures` 3000 EQUALS 120
-- E x 0.04 rounded: 3,000.37 x 0.04 = 120.0148 -> 120.01; 0.125 x 0.04 = 0.005, equidistant -> 0.01.
#ASSERT `reg 5.1(2) — the second additional contribution on the result` `cr1 test figures` 3000.37 EQUALS 120.01
#ASSERT `reg 5.1(2) — the second additional contribution on the result` `cr1 test figures` 0.125 EQUALS 0.01
-- The whole of regulation 5.1, from 2024.
#ASSERT `reg 5.1 — the second additional contribution to deduct from a payment` 2024 `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 68000) `cr1 test no provincial plan` 5000 EQUALS JUST 120
#ASSERT `reg 5.1 — the second additional contribution to deduct from a payment` 2024 `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 72000) `cr1 test no provincial plan` 5000 EQUALS JUST 200
-- The result of (1) must exceed zero: -5,000 and exactly 0 give nothing to deduct, 1 gives 0.04.
#ASSERT `reg 5.1 — the second additional contribution to deduct from a payment` 2024 `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 60000) `cr1 test no provincial plan` 5000 EQUALS JUST 0
#ASSERT `reg 5.1 — the second additional contribution to deduct from a payment` 2024 `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 65000) `cr1 test no provincial plan` 5000 EQUALS JUST 0
#ASSERT `reg 5.1 — the second additional contribution to deduct from a payment` 2024 `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 65000) `cr1 test no provincial plan` 5001 EQUALS JUST 0.04
-- 5.1(3): 380 of the 400 already deducted leaves 20.
#ASSERT `reg 5.1(3) — the room left under the limit on second additional contributions` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test earlier` 0 380 0) `cr1 test no provincial plan` EQUALS JUST 20
#ASSERT `reg 5.1 — the second additional contribution to deduct from a payment` 2024 `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test earlier` 0 380 68000) `cr1 test no provincial plan` 5000 EQUALS JUST 20
-- 6(3): the provincial plan's 100, counted at 50; 300 + 50 deducted leaves 50 of 400.
#ASSERT `reg 5.1(3) — the room left under the limit on second additional contributions` `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test earlier` 0 300 0) (`cr1 test provincial plan` TRUE 800 100 0.10 0.08) EQUALS JUST 50
#ASSERT `reg 5.1 — the second additional contribution to deduct from a payment` 2024 `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test earlier` 0 300 68000) (`cr1 test provincial plan` TRUE 800 100 0.10 0.08) 5000 EQUALS JUST 50
#ASSERT `reg 5.1 — the second additional contribution to deduct from a payment` 2024 `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 68000) (`cr1 test provincial plan` TRUE 800 100 0.10 0.08) 5000 EQUALS JUST 120
-- Before 2024 the regulation does not apply: no answer (not nil).
#ASSERT `reg 5.1 — the second additional contribution to deduct from a payment` 2023 `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 68000) `cr1 test no provincial plan` 5000 EQUALS NOTHING

§§ `Regulations 3 and 4: the employee's contribution to deduct, and regulation 7: the employer's`

-- 6,000 paid monthly: base and first additional (6,000 - 291.66) x 0.06 = 342.5004 -> 342.50; second additional
-- with 68,000 already paid: E = 74,000 - 70,000 = 4,000 x 0.04 = 160.00. Together 502.50.
#ASSERT `reg 4 — the employee's contribution to deduct from a payment` 2024 `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 6000 6000 `paid monthly` 30 12) (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 68000) `cr1 test no provincial plan` EQUALS JUST 502.50
-- The same payment in 2023: only the base and first additional contributions.
#ASSERT `reg 4 — the employee's contribution to deduct from a payment` 2023 `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 6000 6000 `paid monthly` 30 12) (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 68000) `cr1 test no provincial plan` EQUALS JUST 342.50
-- Below the earnings ceiling the second additional contribution is nil.
#ASSERT `reg 4 — the employee's contribution to deduct from a payment` 2024 `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 3000 3000 `paid monthly` 30 12) (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 10000) `cr1 test no provincial plan` EQUALS JUST 162.50
-- A provincial plan with rates that sum to nothing: no answer.
#ASSERT `reg 4 — the employee's contribution to deduct from a payment` 2024 `cr1 test figures` (`cr1 test payment` 3000 3000 `paid monthly` 30 12) (`cr1 test earlier` 0 0 10000) (`cr1 test provincial plan` TRUE 800 100 0 0.08) EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `reg 7 — the employer's contribution to remit in respect of a payment` (JUST 502.50) EQUALS JUST 502.50
#ASSERT `reg 7 — the employer's contribution to remit in respect of a payment` NOTHING EQUALS NOTHING

§§ `Regulation 6.1: salary and wages on which a contribution was made under a provincial pension plan`

#ASSERT `reg 6.1(1) — the salary and wages on which a base contribution has been made under a provincial pension plan` 590 0.059 EQUALS JUST 10000
#ASSERT `reg 6.1(1) — the salary and wages on which a base contribution has been made under a provincial pension plan` 590 0 EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `reg 6.1(2) — the salary and wages on which a first additional contribution has been made under a provincial pension plan` 100 0.01 EQUALS JUST 10000
#ASSERT `reg 6.1(2) — the salary and wages on which a first additional contribution has been made under a provincial pension plan` 100 0 EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `reg 6.1(3) — the salary and wages on which a second additional contribution has been made under a provincial pension plan` 80 0.04 EQUALS JUST 2000
#ASSERT `reg 6.1(3) — the salary and wages on which a second additional contribution has been made under a provincial pension plan` 80 0 EQUALS NOTHING
